ary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"#### Are you using Python or Rust?\r\n\r\nPython\r\n\r\n\r\n#### What version of polars are you using?\r\n\r\n0.10.25\r\n\r\n#### What operating system are you using polars on?\r\n\r\nW10\r\n\r\n#### Describe your bug.\r\nTrying to cover some final gaps in our test coverage. It seems to me  that this should work, given the code that handles strings explicitly, and that it does work on `pl.Series`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef test_add_string() -> None:\r\n    df = pl.DataFrame({\"a\": [\"hi\", \"there\"], \"b\": [\"hello\", \"world\"]})\r\n    result = df + \" hello\"\r\n    expected = pl.DataFrame({\"a\": [\"hi hello\", \"there hello\"], \"b\": [\"hello hello\", \"world hello\"]})\r\n    assert result.frame_equal(expected)\r\n```\r\nIt also returns nearly the right output, except for the column names:\r\n\r\n```\r\n>>> result\r\nshape: (2, 2)\r\n┌─────────────┬─────────────┐\r\n│             ┆             │\r\n│ ---         ┆ ---         │\r\n│ str         ┆ str         │\r\n╞═════════════╪═════════════╡\r\n│ hi hello    ┆ hello hello │\r\n├╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌┼╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌╌┤\r\n│ there hello ┆ world hello │\r\n└─────────────┴─────────────┘\r\n```\r\nWhereas for numeric operations, the column names are preserved.","closed_by":{"login":"ritchie46","id":3023000,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwMjMwMDA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3023000?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ritchie46","html_url":"https://github.com/ritchie46","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ritchie46/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ritchie46/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ritchie46/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ritchie46/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ritchie46/subscriptio